{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying.</b> Israel were commanded in this commandment through women. And it is possible to say the reason for this is that since death is the cause of inheritances, and women brought death to the world, therefore the section of inheritances was fitting to be written through women, because inheritance takes effect among the living by reason of the dead. And the reason the daughters of Tzelofehad merited this is because they cherished the Land, which is called 'the land of YHWH,' designated corresponding to 'the land of the living.'" "<b>If a man die and has no son, then you shall pass his inheritance to his daughter.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said: if he has a son, the son takes precedence to inherit everything; and so a son's daughter, for however many generations, takes precedence over a firstborn daughter; and there is no need to say 'to his seed'; and this is what is said in Bava Batra, chapter 'Yesh Nochelin' — '<b>and has no son</b> (<u>ובן אין לו</u>),' look into it, that is, we prove from the extra yod, for it should have said <u>אן</u> without a yod, in the manner that is written (Deuteronomy 25:7) <u>מאן יבמי</u>, which is without a yod; rather, from the extra yod our Rabbis of blessed memory wished to expound that one must investigate whether there remains for a son after him any name of seed at all."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The printed text reads 'ואין צ\"ל לזרעה' ('and there is no need to say 'to his seed''); this appears to be a parenthetical remark addressing why Scripture does not add the phrase 'to his seed.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:28.347608280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}